WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.790
Where is the acid?

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:12.160
My staff hears me say this more
than they probably care to remember.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.670
But, "where is the acid?"
is really the central

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:20.110
question when we create a new dish.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:27.550
When seasoning foods, everyone
talks about salt. I believe acid

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:32.830
is just as important, maybe
more important than salt.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:38.330
When I grew up in Switzerland, I
was always drawn to acidic foods.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.000
I love tomatoes.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.200
I love citrus fruits.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.300
I love pickles.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:49.330
My mom's cooking-- sauerkraut, roasted
chicken with lemon and rosemary.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:55.970
She made a braised rabbit with
olives, orange, and pickled onions.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:00.790
I never knew exactly why I
was drawn to these dishes

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.400
so much, why they were my favorite.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.560
But looking back, it
makes sense, because there

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:12.520
was presence of acid, which gave it
balance, and it highlighted the food.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.400
And it just makes food better.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.980
Acid just makes food better.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.880
At Eleven Madison, we serve
tasting menus-- 14, 15, 16 courses.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:26.290
It's important that all
these dishes have acid.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.080
We use acid from so
many different sources.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.640
But it keeps the guests excited,
it keeps the meal lighter,

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.160
and it makes the guests want more.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.700
So, what is an acid?

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.470
An acid, by definition, is a molecule
that releases a hydrogen ion.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:53.830
And so here is an image of citric
acid releasing a hydrogen ion.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.655
And this measure of
how strong an acid is,

00:01:57.655 --> 00:02:02.900
is a measure of how easily an
acid gives up that hydrogen ion.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:08.440
And so, for example, citric acid gives
up the hydrogen ion not as easily

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.980
as, say, sulfuric acid does,
which we use in car batteries.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.270
The hydrogen ion is
taken up by the water,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.770
and it's giving us-- instead of an
H2O, it's giving us an H3O plus.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.190
And this is usually referred
to as a hydronium ion.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.760
So, how acidic something
is, then, is a measure

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.560
of how easily hydrogen
ions are given off.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.050
And we usually also refer
to hydrogen ions as protons.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.410
And the more hydrogen
ions and, indirectly,

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.350
how many hydronium
ions there are around,

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.410
is what gives us a measure
of how acidic something is.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.070
So, it turns out that the number
of hydrogen ions in water,

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:53.160
naturally, is 10 to the power
of negative 7 moles per liter,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.620
in a liter of water.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:00.980
In fact, there is 55 moles
of molecules per liter.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.930
And so 10 to the negative 7, then,
is actually a very, very tiny amount.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:09.050
But, nevertheless, this is how many
hydrogen ions there are in water.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.470
Water is thought of
being neutral, and acids

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.450
are molecules that easily give up
hydrogen ions, and so they're acidic.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.490
And so, then, at the
other end of the spectrum,

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:21.910
we have molecules that
are very, very reluctant

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.120
to give up hydrogen ions,
even more so than water.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.680
And those we think of as
bases, or basic molecules.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.410
And there is a mathematical
formula for this,

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:39.640
which is pH equals minus the logarithm
of how many moles per liter of hydrogen

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.050
ions we have.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.680
And so if we do this for water, where
we know that the number of hydrogen

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:49.070
ions 10 to the negative 7, and
we plug this into this formula,

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:53.046
we say minus the logarithm
of 10 to the negative 7.

00:03:53.046 --> 00:03:55.420
And for those of you who are
comfortable with logarithms,

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.790
you immediately see that
the answer to this is 7.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.580
And if you're not, you can
plug this into your calculator

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.190
and actually see that
the number for this is 7.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.770
And so the pH of water is 7.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:09.250
And this is something that a lot
of us are very familiar with,

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:12.020
and if not, this is a good reminder.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.910
We're using a pH meter
at the restaurant.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:24.160
And we're using it as a measure to give
us more consistency, more accuracy.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.600
We're using it in a way we use a scale,
or in a way we measure temperature.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.540
So we took our still water, and this
is the water we serve with our meal,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.520
and we measured it at 7.5.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.250
7 is neutral, so it's
very close to neutral.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.840
We also looked at the
manufactured foods.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.880
Coca-Cola is more acidic
than red wine vinegar.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:47.680
Just imagine that.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.940
Isn't that crazy?

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.140
You eat that with your meal.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.780
It's more acidic than red wine vinegar.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.390
That's unbelievable.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:00.770
And when you drink Coca-Cola, you
never think of it as being acidic.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:10.030
And that's because there's so much
sugar to balance all that acid,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:14.550
and makes it a beverage
that everyone loves.

